<body>
\1\n    <div id="site-header"></div>
    <!-- Header -->
    
    <main class="lesson-container py-4">
        <!-- Breadcrumb -->
        <div id="tutorial-header"></div>
        
        <div class="content lesson-content" style="font-size: 1.05rem; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--text-secondary);">

            <p>First-hop redundancy addresses a fundamental vulnerability in local networks: clients are typically configured with a single <a href="what-is-a-default-gateway.html">default gateway IP address</a>. If that physical router fails, all clients on that subnet lose connectivity outside their VLAN.</p>
            
            <h3>The Gateway Redundancy Concept</h3>
            <p>To solve this, multiple physical routers collaborate to share a <strong>Virtual Gateway IP address</strong> and a virtual MAC address. The clients are configured to use this Virtual IP as their gateway.</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Active / Master Router:</strong> Actively responds to traffic sent to the virtual IP.</li>
                <li><strong>Standby / Backup Router:</strong> Listens for keepalives from the Master. If the Master fails, the Backup assumes the virtual IP and takes over routing seamlessly.</li>
            </ul>
            
            <h4>Generic Example</h4>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Client VLAN:</strong> 192.168.10.0/24</li>
                <li><strong>Router A (Physical IP):</strong> 192.168.10.2</li>
                <li><strong>Router B (Physical IP):</strong> 192.168.10.3</li>
                <li><strong>Virtual Gateway IP (Client Config):</strong> 192.168.10.1</li>
            </ul>
            
            <h3>VRRP vs. HSRP</h3>
            <p>There are two primary protocols used for this:</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>VRRP (Virtual Router Redundancy Protocol):</strong> An open, standards-based protocol supported by nearly all enterprise networking vendors.</li>
                <li><strong>HSRP (Hot Standby Router Protocol):</strong> A historically proprietary protocol developed by Cisco, though deeply entrenched in Cisco environments.</li>
            </ul>
            
            <h3>Advanced Mechanics</h3>
            <p>Behavior is managed through a <strong>priority</strong> value; the router with the highest priority becomes active. A feature called <strong>preemption</strong> dictates whether a higher-priority router automatically takes back control if it recovers from a failure. Finally, <strong>tracking</strong> allows a router to decrement its priority if its upstream WAN link fails, forcing a failover even if the local LAN interface remains online.</p>
        
        </div>
        
